{
  "concept": "A hash function h maps each actual key to a slot of table T, and when several keys collide they are simply strung together in that slot's linked list — so the choice of h alone decides how evenly the keys spread and how long the chains get.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Large outer dark-gray ellipse labeled 'U (universe of keys)'",
    "Inner light-gray ellipse labeled 'K (actual keys)' nested inside U",
    "Eight filled black dots inside K labeled k1…k8, each with its numeric key value shown on hover/next to the label",
    "Vertical column of table slots in the center, labeled 'T' above the top slot",
    "Empty slots drawn as dark-gray rectangles with a diagonal slash (NIL)",
    "Occupied slots drawn as white/light rectangles containing a short horizontal stub line that becomes the head pointer",
    "Slot index labels 0..m-1 running down the left edge of T",
    "Curved/straight arrows from each key dot in K to its slot in T (the hash function h made visible)",
    "Four doubly linked chains to the right of T, one per occupied slot",
    "Each list node drawn as a three-cell box: left prev-pointer cell, center key-label cell (k1, k4, …), right next-pointer cell",
    "Forward arrows (next) and backward arrows (prev) between consecutive nodes in a chain",
    "Diagonal slash in the prev cell of the head node and the next cell of the tail node to mark NIL",
    "Head arrow from each occupied slot into the first node of its chain",
    "Chain-length badge next to each chain (e.g. '2', '3', '1', '2')",
    "Load-factor readout α = n/m displayed near T",
    "Caption/legend: slash = NIL, gray = empty slot"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "hashCode",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "label": "Define the hash function and insert every key",
      "teaches": "That the entire chain structure — which slots are empty, which are long — is produced by one expression h(k), and that keys are prepended to a chain in insertion order",
      "entry_point": "hashAll(keys)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "m()", "does": "returns the number of slots in T (set by the table-size slider)" },
        { "op": "insert(k, i)", "does": "prepends key k to the chain at slot i and draws its arrow from K — the ONLY way to change the figure; errors if i is not an integer in [0, m())" },
        { "op": "chainLength(i)", "does": "returns how many keys currently sit in slot i (read-only)" },
        { "op": "note(msg)", "does": "attaches your own comment to the current frame" }
      ],
      "frame": "the whole figure after one op: the K circle with the arrow just drawn for the current key highlighted, the slot it landed in flashing, and that slot's chain redrawn with the new head node",
      "input": { "label": "Actual keys (numeric values of k1…k8)", "default": "11, 25, 17, 41, 15, 28, 35, 18" },
      "sample_code": "function hashAll(keys) {\n  for (const k of keys) {\n    const i = k % m();        // division method\n    note('h(' + k + ') = ' + i);\n    insert(k, i);\n  }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function hashAll(keys) {\n  for (const k of keys) {\n    // BUG: table size hardcoded as 10 instead of m(),\n    // so slots go out of range (or are never used) when m changes\n    const i = k % 10;\n    insert(k, i);\n  }\n}",
      "success_check": "every input key appears in exactly one chain, and each key k sits in slot h(k) with 0 <= h(k) < m()"
    },
    {
      "id": "tableSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Table size m (number of slots)",
      "teaches": "How the same keys and the same expression k % m() redistribute as m changes — a bad m (e.g. a multiple of a pattern in the keys) piles keys into few chains and drives the load factor up",
      "range": [4, 16, 1],
      "default": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "highlightChain",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Which keys in K collided, by lighting up one slot together with every key dot and arrow that maps to it — searching that slot means walking exactly that chain"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The universe and the actual keys",
      "narration": "U holds every key that could ever occur; only the handful in K are really stored. The table T has far fewer slots than |U|, so some keys must share.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 10 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Run h(k) = k mod m",
      "narration": "The sample code walks the keys and calls insert(k, k % m()). Watch each arrow get drawn one at a time: k1 and k4 both land in slot 1, so the second one is simply prepended in front of the first.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 10 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A collision is just a longer list",
      "narration": "Slot 5 receives three keys and holds a chain of three nodes; slot 7 holds one; five slots stay NIL. Chaining never rejects a key — it only lengthens a list.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 10 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink the table",
      "narration": "Drop m to 5 and re-run: the same eight keys crowd into fewer slots, chains double in length and the load factor α = n/m jumps — search time follows chain length, not table size.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A bad hash function",
      "narration": "Load the buggy version (or try 'return 0'): every key funnels into one slot and the hash table degenerates into a single linked list with Θ(n) search. Then fix h and watch the chains flatten out again.",
      "state": { "tableSize": 10 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Chaining makes collisions harmless but not free — the hash function is the only thing standing between a table of short constant-time chains and a single long list.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three-band layout left→right. Left: two concentric ellipses, outer filled medium gray labeled 'U (universe of keys)' at top, inner filled light gray labeled 'K (actual keys)' at left; eight small filled black dots scattered in the inner ellipse, italic subscripted labels k1…k8 placed just left of or below each dot, with the numeric key value shown in small type. Center: a narrow vertical stack of equal-height rectangles forming T, title 'T' above; empty slots filled dark gray with a thin diagonal slash, occupied slots filled near-white with a short horizontal pointer stub; small index numbers 0..m-1 outside the left edge; an α = n/m readout beneath. Thin black straight arrows run from each key dot across to the left edge of its slot, crossing each other freely — crossings are the visual signature of the hash. Right: for each occupied slot, a horizontal chain of three-cell node boxes (light gray fill, black strokes) with the key label centered; forward next-arrows along the top between boxes and backward prev-arrows along the bottom, diagonal slashes marking NIL in the head's prev cell and the tail's next cell. Highlighting a slot on click should tint the slot, its arrows, its key dots in K, and its whole chain in a single accent color (e.g. orange) while everything else dims. Newly inserted nodes flash at the head of the chain during code replay."
}

